1
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:05,800
Developers love freedom, and security teams love control.

2
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:08,800
How do you give engineers fast, flexible,

3
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:12,699
and powerful development environment that too without letting

4
00:00:12,699 --> 00:00:15,189
code or credential leak to the personal laptops?

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:18,559
The answer is Cloud Workstation and Cloud Shell.

6
00:00:18,649 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's see how we can do that.

7
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:25,215
In modern DevOps, developers often need access to the production like APIs,

8
00:00:25,625 --> 00:00:27,215
access to private repos,

9
00:00:27,274 --> 00:00:32,395
and also sometimes they need a local like ID experience. But

10
00:00:32,395 --> 00:00:34,534
when we do it on our traditional laptops,

11
00:00:34,794 --> 00:00:37,154
that creates the risk of source code leak,

12
00:00:37,395 --> 00:00:39,845
security issues like secrets stored locally.

13
00:00:39,994 --> 00:00:45,744
So to answer that, what you can do is you can create a cloud‑based workstation.

14
00:00:45,944 --> 00:00:47,235
Type in the first command.

15
00:00:47,669 --> 00:00:52,029
I'm going to say gcloud workstations cluster, and create the cluster.

16
00:00:52,189 --> 00:00:56,110
Now I'm giving the name of this cluster as a dev cluster.

17
00:00:56,229 --> 00:01:00,689
I need to provide the region, network, and subnetwork information.

18
00:01:00,830 --> 00:01:02,000
And let's say Enter.

19
00:01:02,159 --> 00:01:02,930
Now remember,

20
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:05,503
when you're going to create the cluster, this is

21
00:01:05,503 --> 00:01:09,984
going to take a couple of minutes, almost 15 to 20 minutes it is going to take,

22
00:01:10,183 --> 00:01:13,444
so we have to run this command and wait patiently.

23
00:01:13,694 --> 00:01:15,374
Now my cluster got created.

24
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:17,543
If I want to go and see it,

25
00:01:17,704 --> 00:01:22,344
I can come down here onto the Google Cloud and search for workstation.

26
00:01:22,823 --> 00:01:26,524
So let's go to the Workstations, and here under the Workstation,

27
00:01:26,664 --> 00:01:31,624
I can see Cluster management. And inside the cluster manager, you can see

28
00:01:31,624 --> 00:01:37,160
that we have the dev cluster ready and it has taken up all the network and

29
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:40,319
subnetwork information that I have provided.

30
00:01:40,519 --> 00:01:45,989
It is not that you can only create this cluster from the command.

31
00:01:46,199 --> 00:01:50,610
You can also create this cluster directly from the Google Cloud from here on.

32
00:01:51,139 --> 00:01:54,120
You can just come down here and say Create,

33
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:57,779
and then you can provide all the information like name,

34
00:01:58,569 --> 00:02:02,709
location, and the network setting, whatever you like to give,

35
00:02:03,019 --> 00:02:04,599
and then you can say Create.

36
00:02:04,739 --> 00:02:08,649
So this Cloud Workstation cluster can be created through the

37
00:02:08,649 --> 00:02:11,070
command line, as well as through here.

38
00:02:11,509 --> 00:02:13,869
Now, as we have already created the cluster,

39
00:02:14,139 --> 00:02:18,300
now it's time to go ahead and set some kind of a configuration for that.

40
00:02:18,539 --> 00:02:22,339
For that, I'm going to again use gcloud command.

41
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:25,820
Let me say clear here and type in the command.

42
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:28,690
So the command for creating the Workstations

43
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:32,419
configuration is gcloud workstations config,

44
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:36,270
and then you say create and give the configuration name.

45
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:40,990
I'm giving the configuration name as payments‑config.

46
00:02:41,210 --> 00:02:43,800
I provide the region, cluster.

47
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:48,580
Remember this is the cluster which we have created in the previous step.

48
00:02:48,899 --> 00:02:52,059
So I'm giving the same cluster that is the dev cluster.

49
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,199
I'm providing the machine type,

50
00:02:54,419 --> 00:02:58,919
saying that when you're going to add the workstation using this configuration,

51
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:04,440
the machine size of that would be e2‑standard‑2,

52
00:03:04,619 --> 00:03:08,740
and then I'm providing lastly the container image.

53
00:03:08,940 --> 00:03:12,610
So what could be the image of container, that is

54
00:03:12,610 --> 00:03:14,539
going to get loaded in that machine.

55
00:03:14,770 --> 00:03:18,929
So I'm using some predefined image, and let's say Enter.

56
00:03:19,210 --> 00:03:22,429
Now this is also going to take a couple of more minutes

57
00:03:22,610 --> 00:03:25,520
to go and create this configuration.

58
00:03:25,809 --> 00:03:30,770
Now the same configuration you can go back and see from the UI as well.

59
00:03:30,970 --> 00:03:32,589
So you minimize this window,

60
00:03:32,889 --> 00:03:37,050
go to the Workstation configurations, and here you can see

61
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:40,740
that configuration once this gets added up properly. Right

62
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:45,740
now it is in the state of updating, so it is yet not fully created.

63
00:03:45,990 --> 00:03:47,960
Meanwhile, that is running behind the scene.

64
00:03:48,179 --> 00:03:50,600
We can go inside this payment config.

65
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:53,460
That is the configuration. Here you can see that

66
00:03:53,669 --> 00:03:55,820
right now the status is Updating,

67
00:03:56,179 --> 00:04:00,300
but it contains the information that we have provided,

68
00:04:00,309 --> 00:04:02,460
like the cluster name is dev‑cluster.

69
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:03,899
The machine setting,

70
00:04:03,979 --> 00:04:09,300
it is still updating, and correspondingly, we can add a couple of more

71
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:14,619
information in this configuration part. And you can also click on Add users

72
00:04:14,619 --> 00:04:18,000
and the permission to that specific configuration.

73
00:04:18,238 --> 00:04:22,489
In fact, you can also create the configuration from here on as well.

74
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:24,950
You can just say here +Create,

75
00:04:25,019 --> 00:04:28,220
and then you can create the workstation configuration from

76
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:30,940
here also. And when you do it from here,

77
00:04:31,149 --> 00:04:33,399
you can give further more information,

78
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:37,059
very specifically. At the time of typing the command,

79
00:04:37,179 --> 00:04:40,179
we might not have given that detailed information,

80
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:44,739
but here you can set a couple of more information as well.

81
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:47,279
So if you go back to our terminal,

82
00:04:47,459 --> 00:04:52,579
you can see that it is showing that configuration has been created successfully.

83
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:55,769
Now, once the configuration has been created successfully,

84
00:04:55,989 --> 00:04:58,570
it's time to create the workstation.

85
00:04:58,829 --> 00:05:01,290
So let's type in one more command for that.

86
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:07,049
So the command is gcloud workstations and we are saying create the workstation,

87
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:11,109
giving the workstation name as payment‑ws.

88
00:05:11,339 --> 00:05:16,350
We provide the region and the cluster and the config which we have just

89
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:21,570
created so that this workstation automatically takes all the properties

90
00:05:21,570 --> 00:05:23,989
that you have set into the configuration.

91
00:05:24,269 --> 00:05:24,950
Say Enter.

92
00:05:25,190 --> 00:05:29,190
Now it will take a couple of more minutes, and then it will create. So

93
00:05:29,190 --> 00:05:32,250
you can see that we've got our workstation ready.

94
00:05:33,799 --> 00:05:35,730
We can come down into our UI part again,

95
00:05:35,989 --> 00:05:37,950
go down to the Cloud Workstations,

96
00:05:38,109 --> 00:05:41,230
and here we can click on this Workstations tab.

97
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:46,470
And within the Workstations, you can see that we have one workstation ready.

98
00:05:46,790 --> 00:05:50,429
So this workstation right now in the Stopped state,

99
00:05:51,059 --> 00:05:54,450
either you can run a command to start that workstation,

100
00:05:54,630 --> 00:05:57,899
or you can start it from here saying Start.

101
00:05:58,109 --> 00:06:02,910
Now this will also take a couple of minutes to get started and ready.

102
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:05,369
Now once this gets started and ready,

103
00:06:05,510 --> 00:06:10,950
it will be available for us to directly use it. You will get one URL.

104
00:06:11,190 --> 00:06:16,339
With the help of that URL, you can launch this specific Cloud Workstation.

105
00:06:16,579 --> 00:06:20,309
Now you can see that our workstation is in the Running state.

106
00:06:20,549 --> 00:06:22,649
I can again click on these three dots,

107
00:06:22,670 --> 00:06:26,989
and if I want to see the specific logs related to that workstation,

108
00:06:27,109 --> 00:06:32,429
I can click on Logs, and that will show me all the state it has gone through,

109
00:06:32,670 --> 00:06:35,350
and you can see the entire information.

110
00:06:35,589 --> 00:06:40,709
If you go back again and let's say you want to get into this workstation,

111
00:06:40,910 --> 00:06:45,029
so you have to just simply click on this workstation and on the

112
00:06:45,029 --> 00:06:48,190
top here you can see an option of Launch.

113
00:06:48,390 --> 00:06:49,640
So you can launch this,

114
00:06:49,750 --> 00:06:53,829
or maybe you want to connect to the web app on a specific

115
00:06:53,829 --> 00:06:56,670
port or you want to connect via SSH.

116
00:06:56,989 --> 00:07:01,429
So let's do the connect to the web app, and I got this URL.

117
00:07:01,709 --> 00:07:07,649
What I can do is I can copy this URL and open it in another tab.

118
00:07:07,950 --> 00:07:12,109
Now you will see that we got an entire workstation.

119
00:07:12,309 --> 00:07:16,239
So this is a clean environment, we get it.

120
00:07:16,390 --> 00:07:18,989
I can say let's just open a folder,

121
00:07:19,109 --> 00:07:25,230
and from here I can start doing my work on this newly created Cloud Workstation.

122
00:07:25,619 --> 00:07:29,269
So that will be a clean environment where your developer can

123
00:07:29,269 --> 00:07:33,989
work and everything will reside in this machine rather than on

124
00:07:33,989 --> 00:07:36,109
the developer's personal laptop.

125
00:07:36,309 --> 00:07:39,269
You also get the terminal, click on these three lines,

126
00:07:39,589 --> 00:07:41,670
say Terminal and say New Terminal.

127
00:07:41,869 --> 00:07:44,130
So in the bottom, you get the terminal.

128
00:07:44,299 --> 00:07:46,130
Let me just a little bit expand it.

129
00:07:46,309 --> 00:07:50,230
If you want to install further more items, you can install it from here.

130
00:07:50,309 --> 00:07:53,010
You can just say sudo aptget update.

131
00:07:54,649 --> 00:07:56,869
Now this update gets done, let me do clear,

132
00:07:57,089 --> 00:08:01,089
and then what I can do is I can keep on installing

133
00:08:01,089 --> 00:08:04,450
furthermore libraries like Google Cloud CLI,

134
00:08:04,730 --> 00:08:10,510
maybe kubectl, Terraform, and all I can do it on this specific workstation.

135
00:08:10,690 --> 00:08:13,730
Besides this specific Cloud Workstation,

136
00:08:13,970 --> 00:08:16,750
we also have an option of a Cloud Shell.

137
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:20,510
Now Cloud Shell is something that is always available,

138
00:08:20,679 --> 00:08:22,825
and lightweight dev terminal.

139
00:08:23,005 --> 00:08:26,904
Although we are already using it since the beginning of this course,

140
00:08:27,144 --> 00:08:28,864
I'll just walk you through it again.

141
00:08:29,065 --> 00:08:32,304
When you go down to the Google Cloud, on the right top corner,

142
00:08:32,424 --> 00:08:34,414
you see a sign for the terminal.

143
00:08:34,655 --> 00:08:36,344
Right now my terminal is already open.

144
00:08:36,664 --> 00:08:40,234
Let me just from the bottom, let me close this window.

145
00:08:40,385 --> 00:08:44,664
So here on the top, you start to again see this terminal option enabled.

146
00:08:44,825 --> 00:08:47,905
You can click on this, and you will get your Cloud Shell.

147
00:08:48,145 --> 00:08:50,460
Now the beauty of this Cloud Shell is that you can

148
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:53,369
run any command that you want,

149
00:08:53,559 --> 00:08:56,520
and you don't have to install anything specifically.

150
00:08:56,799 --> 00:09:02,469
All your Google Cloud SDK and CLI is already installed here.

151
00:09:02,679 --> 00:09:07,020
In fact, if you want to have that kind of a Visual Studio Code,

152
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:11,380
you can click on this Open editor and you get in an environment

153
00:09:11,559 --> 00:09:15,869
where you can get an IDE like Visual Studio Code.

154
00:09:16,150 --> 00:09:22,294
This Cloud Shell is given to as a free VM, and it is pre‑installed with gcloud,

155
00:09:22,455 --> 00:09:24,494
kubectl, and Terraform.

156
00:09:24,684 --> 00:09:28,955
It also consists of 5 GB of persistent storage,

157
00:09:29,094 --> 00:09:33,854
and most importantly, it is secure, browser‑based as such,

158
00:09:34,054 --> 00:09:37,414
and it does not require any local credentials.

159
00:09:37,664 --> 00:09:42,174
So that's how the Cloud Workstation and Cloud Shell provide a secure,

160
00:09:42,234 --> 00:09:45,594
scalable, production‑ready developer environment.
